      "content": "Sure, I can certainly help you with that depreciation calculation! You can think of the equipment as a very expensive, slightly grumpy goblin that's going to be around for a while before it decides to retire.\n\nTo do the straight-line depreciation, you first need to figure out the **depreciable base**, which is the cost minus the salvage value. In this case, that's $45,000 minus your $5,000 \"goblin pension\" (salvage value), leaving you with a depreciable base of **$40,000**.\n\nNext, you divide that depreciable base by the useful life of the asset. Since the goblin is expected to work for **8 years**, you take that $40,000 and divide it by 8.\n\nThe math is simple: $40,000 / 8 = **$5,000 per year**.\n\nSo, every year, you record a depreciation expense of $5,000. This is like the goblin slowly losing its teeth and strength, but at a steady, predictable rate until it finally retires in 8 years. If you ever",
